well you know it's going to be a great  hour when we have a doctor in the yeah  yes indeed that's for sure so excited  about that  thank you for joining us but we begin  this hour with a major development in  the fight against the coronavirus pfizer  is asking the fda to authorize its covet  booster for all americans 18 and older  the shots would even be available to  adults who received the moderna or j j  vaccines this is fizer's second attempt  to push boosters for most americans the  fda advisory panel rejected its first  request in august and limited the shots  to people 65 and older and those in  certain high-risk groups but pfizer says  it has new data showing the booster  offers almost complete protection from  severe covet join us right now like i  said u.s surgeon general dr vivek morthy  thank you for joining us yeah i'm glad  to be with you all today of course now  let's just jump right in pfizer says  this new data  it shows that the booster can provide  almost complete protection against the  covid virus um  do all adults need this  well it's a good question and that's  what the fda is going to have to look  into they'll have to examine the data  carefully understand whether giving a  boost to that additional group of people  that advisor is recommending is both  safe and effective but here's what we've  seen already from preliminary results  from uh from israel from their  experience they have seen a significant  increase in protection when they've  given boosters across the population but  the most important thing we've got to  remember right now is if you're eligible  for a booster please go out and get  boosted uh and if you haven't been  vaccinated at all it is imperative that  you get vaccinated because we still have  a lot of covet floating around and  winter is coming which means that  there's a chance uh that as more people  go indoors there's a risk for spread so  how soon can this specific pfizer  booster be available  well the fda will have to go through  their process they've been prioritizing  vaccines so i imagine potentially in the  order of weeks they will make a decision  around this but again it depends on what  they see when they look at that data  yeah pfizer tried to get this done back  in august september based on what was  happening in israel so hopefully it'll  be sooner rather than later can we talk  about the misinformation that's out  there about the vaccines you know we've  got the big story in the news about  aaron rodgers who seems to be using his  platform to spread misinformation how do  you deal with that what are your  thoughts about that  well gail i'm glad you brought it up  because health misinformation is one of  the greatest threats we have right now  in our coveted response and more broadly  for health it's one of the reasons why  in july of this year i issued a surgeon  general's advisory on the dangers of  health misinformation it's also why  yesterday i actually also followed up by  issuing a community toolkit that  families can actually use that community  leaders can use  to give themselves  training and tools to actually fight  misinformation in their own lives but  this is a fundamental problem because i  you know i think people have the right  to make their own decisions but i also  believe as a doctor that they have the  right to have accurate information  you know so they can make the best  decisions for themselves  that he's allergic to  to something that's in the pfizer and  the modern vaccine have you heard of  people being allergic to anything in  this talk about aaron rodgers yes aaron  rodgers yes so there is a very very  small number of people who may have an  allergy to one of the components of the  vaccine this is very rare though uh and  you know in those cases then they can  talk to their doctor and we can they can  look for an alternative vaccine  but again this is exceedingly rare one  of the the things i think to draw though  from this whole example that's been  talked about more recently is that who  you get your information from really  matters yes because somebody has  millions of followers you know in social  media because they happen to have a  well-known name does not mean that they  are a credible  authoritative source when it comes to  vaccine information and especially now  that we have a vaccine that's out for  kids 5-11 it's especially important that  parents know that there's misinformation  out there they talk to their doctor  their department of health their  children's hospital or the cdc to  actually get credible information about  the vaccine so doctor on the subject of  credible information accurate  information i'm a big believer in the  idea that the american people can handle  anything as long as they know what to  expect so on that point what should we  expect medium to long term from the  coronavirus is it now time can you tell  us and acknowledge that this is going to  be around it's never going to be  eradicated and at some point we're going  to have to just be living with it  well great question as we look to the  future i think what is likely to happen  is that there will be coronavirus around  for for some period of time but i do  think we can knock it down in terms of  its severity and second we can reduce  the number of cases so we can get to the  point where we live with it just like  any other respiratory virus that's where  the vaccines have been so helpful keep  in mind if you look at the delta wave we  experienced over the summer the vast  majority of people who had severe  infection were hospitalized or who died  were people who are unvaccinated and  that means again and tells us that these  vaccines are remarkably effective we  also have two new oral medications that  are on the horizon the fda has to review  their data but the initial trials show  that they were very effective for  reducing hospitalization and debt so  it'll be around for some good news to  come good news to come uh and you say  covert will be around for some period of  time i mean should people be prepared  for that period of time to be forever  well i think we can certainly learn to  live with it like think about the common  cold for example like we have learned to  live with the common cold and you know  we can take precautions to protect  ourselves but we know how to get through  it uh we can ultimately get covet i  think to a place where it's somewhere in  between the cold and the flu where yes  it's a virus that's circulating but it  doesn't disrupt our way of life doesn't  prevent us from seeing the people we  love doesn't prevent our kids from being  in school okay thanksgiving coming up  should unvaccinated people be  fraternizing over holiday dinner with  vaccinated people would you do that  asking for a friend asking for wearing  well look i think if you're gathering  indoors with vaccinated and unvaccinated  people together that is certainly a  higher risk situation especially if  you're a parent like me and you've got  unvaccinated kids at home because  they're not eligible yet or they haven't  gotten a vaccine yet then you want to be  especially cautious the other thing  though to remember is there are steps we  can take to reduce risk further wearing  masks indoors helps using testing  strategically can also help to reduce  the risk of people coming to a gathering  infected  all right and children can be vaccinated  over the age of five that that that's  coming you know you're coming i got to  love yeah you need to okay we've got a  long time to get vaccinated  that's a big big word especially for  kids like mine i've got a five-year-old  would be a problem if you said no i'm  not vaccinating mine so  that would be that would be a problem  that would be your problem it's so good  to see you in first of all you're a real  boy we only see you i don't know  so nice to see you thank you very much  so good to be with all of you more thank  you

